WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.800
When you think about the software running on your computer right now,

00:03.380 --> 00:07.460
you know, you probably picture this kind of pure weightless math.

00:07.560 --> 00:10.860
Right, like clean, crisp logic floating somewhere in the cloud.

00:11.000 --> 00:11.520
Exactly.

00:11.860 --> 00:16.300
We tend to visualize it as this pristine binary architecture

00:16.300 --> 00:20.240
that is completely divorced from the messy physical world.

00:20.500 --> 00:23.640
But, uh, it's really not. Like, at all.

00:23.860 --> 00:24.800
No, it's not.

00:25.100 --> 00:28.120
If you peel back the layers of the tools we use to write that code,

00:28.120 --> 00:32.020
or even just, you know, the standard text editors we use to draft a basic email,

00:32.580 --> 00:35.480
that pristine illusion shatters almost immediately.

00:35.620 --> 00:36.700
Oh, yeah, completely shatters.

00:36.820 --> 00:40.000
We are not looking at pure math. We are looking at a haunted house.

00:40.440 --> 00:43.360
And the ghosts that are haunting your keystrokes today

00:43.360 --> 00:47.340
are actually the heavy, clanking iron machines of the 19th century.

00:47.580 --> 00:51.820
Which is just wild. It is the absolute definition of technological residue.

00:51.980 --> 00:52.280
Right.

00:52.420 --> 00:57.100
We are literally carrying the physical, metallic constraints of antique furniture

00:57.100 --> 00:58.840
into the digital age.

00:59.380 --> 01:01.420
And, you know, we're treating those constraints

01:01.420 --> 01:03.620
as if they were fundamental laws of computing.

01:03.620 --> 01:05.240
Which brings us to today.

01:05.700 --> 01:07.320
Welcome to the Deep Dive.

01:07.740 --> 01:12.660
Today, we are pulling from a really fascinating independent research essay.

01:12.800 --> 01:16.640
Yeah, it was published in 2026 by someone writing under the name Flyxion.

01:16.760 --> 01:17.600
Right. And it's titled,

01:17.760 --> 01:21.420
Address Before Operator, From the Typewriter Carriage to Vim's Grammar.

01:21.560 --> 01:22.840
It's a brilliant piece of work.

01:22.840 --> 01:27.340
It really is. The mission here is to trace how the very real,

01:27.520 --> 01:33.840
very heavy constraints of brass, iron, and wood evolved into invisible digital codes.

01:34.120 --> 01:37.960
And, well, how those codes eventually shaped the actual grammar of the software

01:37.960 --> 01:39.640
you and I interact with every single day.

01:39.740 --> 01:43.880
Yeah, we are tracing a direct lineage from a Danish clergyman in the 1870s

01:43.880 --> 01:46.920
all the way to the text editors running on modern cloud servers.

01:47.360 --> 01:48.980
So, okay, let's unpack this.

01:49.040 --> 01:49.480
Let's do it.

01:49.480 --> 01:52.260
Because software syntax didn't start as code, did it?

01:52.320 --> 01:55.420
It started as literal, physical furniture.

01:55.660 --> 01:57.200
No, it didn't start as code at all.

01:57.620 --> 02:00.700
To really grasp the architecture of modern software,

02:01.360 --> 02:06.200
you have to deeply understand the physical world it was desperately trying to mimic, right?

02:06.240 --> 02:06.480
Right.

02:06.700 --> 02:07.800
And ultimately accommodate.

02:08.300 --> 02:11.580
We aren't just looking at the surface-level functions of what computers do today.

02:11.900 --> 02:13.060
We're looking at the why.

02:13.060 --> 02:16.240
Exactly. We are reverse engineering why they do it.

02:16.520 --> 02:21.600
Because the hidden lineage of everyday technology begins with the brutal,

02:22.020 --> 02:24.940
physical realities of early typing machines.

02:25.180 --> 02:28.280
And the journey begins in Denmark right around 1870.

02:28.580 --> 02:31.020
With an inventor named Rasmus Mollinghansen.

02:31.360 --> 02:31.640
Yeah.

02:31.900 --> 02:36.520
And he was actually the principal of the Royal Institute for the Deaf Mutes in Copenhagen,

02:36.740 --> 02:38.500
which I think is crucial context here.

02:38.500 --> 02:39.120
Oh, absolutely.

02:39.500 --> 02:43.520
He was constantly thinking about tactile interfaces and communication.

02:43.920 --> 02:44.040
Right.

02:44.160 --> 02:46.440
How human hands interact with language.

02:46.720 --> 02:48.560
And he invents something called the writing ball.

02:48.820 --> 02:50.280
Which is such a deceptive name.

02:50.380 --> 02:51.140
It really is.

02:51.220 --> 02:55.620
Because, you know, the name makes you picture something small that you just roll around on a desk.

02:55.780 --> 02:58.460
But the reality was vastly more complex.

02:58.800 --> 03:02.000
Vastly more complex and just heavily, heavily engineered.

03:02.000 --> 03:06.180
I mean, the polished production models of the writing ball, the ones that ended up in museums,

03:06.420 --> 03:08.800
they look like a large brass hemisphere.

03:09.100 --> 03:10.820
Like a giant metallic pin cushion, basically.

03:11.360 --> 03:11.660
Yes.

03:12.120 --> 03:14.440
A pin cushion bristling with keys.

03:15.420 --> 03:18.160
54 spring-loaded type bars, to be exact.

03:18.400 --> 03:24.880
And they were arrayed in a half globe, all converging precisely on a single printing point underneath.

03:25.060 --> 03:25.360
Wow.

03:25.360 --> 03:32.500
And while that hemisphere is impressive, the earliest prototype, the one from between 1867 and 1869,

03:32.800 --> 03:37.280
is where you really see this crazy collision of hardware and furniture.

03:37.820 --> 03:37.900
Right.

03:37.980 --> 03:40.660
Because he didn't just build a machine you placed on the table.

03:40.780 --> 03:42.020
The machine was the table.

03:42.140 --> 03:42.520
Exactly.

03:43.100 --> 03:47.260
The initial prototype was seamlessly integrated into a wooden writing desk.

03:48.120 --> 03:52.160
And it utilized a foot pedal to drive the paper cylinder.

03:52.300 --> 03:53.200
Wait, a foot pedal?

03:53.320 --> 03:54.420
Like you're driving a car.

03:54.420 --> 03:54.700
Yeah.

03:55.280 --> 03:58.120
If you think about the physical mechanics required to operate that,

03:58.480 --> 04:02.220
the user is typing with their hands while operating the paper movement with their feet.

04:02.280 --> 04:03.260
That sounds exhausting.

04:03.440 --> 04:06.180
It was like playing a piano or running an industrial sewing machine.

04:06.480 --> 04:11.500
That is the level of physical, full-body mechanical intervention we are talking about at the dawn of typing.

04:11.700 --> 04:15.580
So the machine and the table were just one contiguous, heavy interface.

04:15.840 --> 04:16.280
They were.

04:16.640 --> 04:19.000
But over time, he refines it, you know.

04:19.160 --> 04:22.240
For the production models, they actually get rid of the foot pedal.

04:22.400 --> 04:23.000
Thank goodness.

04:23.220 --> 04:23.620
Right?

04:23.620 --> 04:27.680
And they moved that paper advancing function onto the machine itself.

04:27.840 --> 04:32.260
They created this specific heavy button on the front left of the writing ball.

04:32.500 --> 04:37.980
And the mechanics of this button are where we see the very first seed of modern computing constraints.

04:38.180 --> 04:38.340
Right?

04:38.900 --> 04:41.140
Because pressing it didn't just do one thing.

04:41.140 --> 04:41.460
No.

04:41.820 --> 04:49.300
Pressing that single mechanical button accomplished two critical simultaneous actions in one heavy stroke.

04:49.420 --> 04:49.660
Okay.

04:49.780 --> 04:50.240
What were they?

04:50.860 --> 04:54.700
Well, it forced the curved paper frame concentrically back to the start of the line.

04:54.960 --> 04:58.680
And at the exact same time, it stepped the paper down one line.

04:58.780 --> 04:59.240
Ah.

04:59.240 --> 05:01.740
So returning the carriage and feeding the line.

05:01.800 --> 05:02.200
Exactly.

05:02.520 --> 05:03.800
Returning the carriage, feeding the line.

05:03.880 --> 05:04.000
Right.

05:04.080 --> 05:08.220
One physical motion for the human, two distinct physical results on the paper.

05:08.380 --> 05:09.100
That's fascinating.

05:09.280 --> 05:12.260
And the engineering for this was reportedly phenomenal, wasn't it?

05:12.260 --> 05:13.240
It was world-class.

05:13.240 --> 05:21.640
At the 1878 Paris World's Fair, Molly Hansen's writing ball actually went head-to-head with the Remington Scholes and Glidden Typewriter.

05:21.640 --> 05:26.520
And the Remington, of course, is the granddaddy of the QWERTY keyboards we all use today.

05:26.660 --> 05:27.640
The very same.

05:28.140 --> 05:30.740
And setting the scene at that World's Fair is important.

05:30.880 --> 05:36.380
I mean, you have these grand halls filled with steam engines and early electrical marvels.

05:36.400 --> 05:39.300
Just massive Victorian innovation everywhere.

05:39.640 --> 05:39.880
Right.

05:40.320 --> 05:43.840
And here are these competing visions for how humans will mechanically write.

05:44.200 --> 05:47.200
It was an intense clash of industrial philosophies.

05:47.300 --> 05:48.560
So who went out at the fair?

05:48.560 --> 05:52.580
Well, the journalists and engineers there meticulously compared the two.

05:53.180 --> 05:58.360
And the consensus was actually that the Danish writing ball was superior in several ways.

05:58.820 --> 05:59.080
Really?

05:59.780 --> 06:02.560
The weird brass pincushion beat the Remington.

06:02.940 --> 06:03.400
It did.

06:03.800 --> 06:09.260
It was considered cheaper to manufacture, remarkably solid, and far more precise than the Remington.

06:09.380 --> 06:10.760
Because the Remington was kind of clunky.

06:11.020 --> 06:16.560
Yeah, the Remington mechanism was derived from sewing machine manufacturing, so it was a bit rough by comparison.

06:16.560 --> 06:19.420
But obviously, the Remington ultimately won the long game.

06:19.680 --> 06:22.040
I mean, we don't type on half globes today.

06:22.180 --> 06:22.440
True.

06:22.780 --> 06:28.260
And a huge part of that victory comes down to what the author of the essay calls the case problem.

06:28.600 --> 06:29.320
Ah, yes.

06:29.440 --> 06:30.920
The upper and lower case problem.

06:31.020 --> 06:31.240
Right.

06:31.580 --> 06:44.140
If you are an engineer in the 1870s, you have to figure out how to design a machine to type both a capital A and a lower case A without doubling the physical footprint of the entire machine.

06:44.140 --> 06:47.800
And this is one of the classic mechanical engineering trade-offs of the era.

06:47.940 --> 06:50.820
You have a fixed budget of space and mechanical complexity.

06:51.020 --> 06:51.160
Right.

06:51.220 --> 06:52.780
You only have so much room on a desk.

06:53.000 --> 06:53.380
Exactly.

06:53.540 --> 07:02.000
You need physical strike points, the type bars, to cover an entire alphabet's worth of symbols, numbers, punctuation, plus upper and lower case letters.

07:02.240 --> 07:04.220
So how did the Danish writing ball handle it?

07:04.620 --> 07:07.560
Well, Mollinghansen solved this by brute forcing the problem.

07:08.080 --> 07:12.020
He gave almost every single character its own dedicated key and type bar.

07:12.020 --> 07:14.020
Oh, wow. So that's why it had so many keys.

07:14.440 --> 07:19.720
That is precisely why the writing ball required that massive, complex hemisphere of over 50 keys.

07:20.300 --> 07:25.760
He traded a simple, manageable keyboard layout for a much larger, more complex mechanical ray.

07:25.880 --> 07:28.180
Okay, is this basically like packing for a trip?

07:28.600 --> 07:34.940
You know, you either bring a giant suitcase with a highly specific tool for every single job, like the writing ball,

07:35.100 --> 07:40.480
or you bring a tiny bag with a multi-tool that you have to physically adjust to change its function.

07:40.480 --> 07:42.360
That is a perfect analogy.

07:42.860 --> 07:48.380
If you want a capital A on the writing ball, you press the specific brass rod dedicated to the capital A.

07:48.660 --> 07:53.060
If you want a lowercase a, you find the entirely different rod for that.

07:53.240 --> 07:54.060
The giant suitcase.

07:54.580 --> 07:54.820
Right.

07:55.140 --> 07:56.760
But it requires a lot of real estate.

07:57.340 --> 08:00.980
The American machines, like the Remingtons, took the multi-tool approach.

08:01.320 --> 08:04.120
They wanted to keep the keyboard relatively small and easy to learn.

08:04.120 --> 08:11.660
So to achieve that, they had to share one type bar between two different characters, usually an uppercase and lowercase version of the same letter.

08:11.760 --> 08:12.160
Exactly.

08:12.960 --> 08:18.560
But to make one piece of metal print two different things, the machine has to physically change its state.

08:18.660 --> 08:23.000
Meaning the paper and the ribbon have to align with a different part of the metal type bar.

08:23.000 --> 08:27.800
And changing that state required shifting heavy metal mass.

08:28.040 --> 08:28.240
Oh.

08:28.360 --> 08:36.300
You're either physically tilting the entire heavy paper carriage upward, or in later designs, you were dropping the heavy metal type basket.

08:36.440 --> 08:39.580
The type basket being the internal assembly holding all the type bars.

08:39.640 --> 08:39.860
Right.

08:39.940 --> 08:40.960
This big chunk of metal.

08:41.400 --> 08:45.160
And that action is the literal origin of the shift key.

08:45.580 --> 08:47.660
It wasn't just a digital modifier like it is today.

08:47.780 --> 08:48.940
It was a physical lever.

08:48.940 --> 08:56.040
A literal physical lever that you pressed with your left pinky finger to hoist or drop a substantial mass of iron and brass.

08:56.180 --> 08:57.580
That sounds brutal on the hand.

08:57.820 --> 09:02.660
I mean, going back to the packing analogy, the Remington is the tiny bag with the multi-tool.

09:02.800 --> 09:03.580
It's much more compact.

09:03.940 --> 09:11.480
But every single time you want to use the screwdriver instead of the knife, you have to physically stop and forcefully lock the tool into a new state.

09:11.660 --> 09:11.880
Yes.

09:11.980 --> 09:17.140
And doing that with your pinky finger hundreds of times a day has a serious biological cost, doesn't it?

09:17.140 --> 09:18.740
A significant biological cost.

09:19.340 --> 09:20.600
Profound human fatigue.

09:21.380 --> 09:26.400
The multi-tool approach won out because a smaller keyboard was easier to standardize for touch typing.

09:26.920 --> 09:28.900
But holding that shift key down was awful.

09:29.200 --> 09:29.800
I can imagine.

09:30.320 --> 09:34.780
Just suspending that heavy metal carriage in midair with just a pinky finger.

09:35.120 --> 09:36.400
It was incredibly tiring.

09:36.600 --> 09:36.960
Uh-huh.

09:37.040 --> 09:42.300
Especially if you needed to type a full word, let alone an entire title, in capital letters.

09:42.300 --> 09:47.860
So the physical constraint of the human hand forced the engineers to invent a workaround, right?

09:48.240 --> 09:51.940
Because a typist just couldn't physically hold that mechanism up for long stretches.

09:52.160 --> 09:52.340
Right.

09:52.460 --> 10:00.020
So the manufacturers added a mechanical catch, a little physical latch that would grab the heavy carriage and hold it in the shifted position.

10:00.080 --> 10:01.800
Until you pressed it again to release it.

10:01.800 --> 10:02.300
Exactly.

10:02.420 --> 10:04.380
They created a physical shift lock.

10:04.380 --> 10:09.120
And this is where the essay's idea of formal residue becomes really undeniable.

10:09.820 --> 10:13.120
What's fascinating here is how that physical lock transitions over time.

10:13.900 --> 10:19.820
Fast forward a few decades, typewriters become electric, and eventually they evolve into computer keyboards.

10:19.820 --> 10:23.800
And the massive heavy iron carriage disappears entirely.

10:24.100 --> 10:25.820
The heavy metal type basket disappears.

10:26.560 --> 10:30.260
Everything is replaced by lightweight electronic switches and digital logic.

10:30.460 --> 10:32.800
The physical lock had absolutely nothing left to hold.

10:32.900 --> 10:36.860
There was no mass to suspend anymore, but the ghost remains.

10:37.240 --> 10:40.180
The iron is gone, but the ghost is there.

10:40.840 --> 10:45.820
Because the behavioral expectation, the human habit of locking the uppercase state,

10:45.820 --> 10:52.080
was so deeply ingrained in office culture and typing pedagogy that it just couldn't be removed.

10:52.180 --> 10:53.440
People still wanted that function.

10:53.580 --> 10:53.780
Yeah.

10:53.980 --> 10:55.960
So it was reborn as a software toggle.

10:56.040 --> 10:56.260
Ah.

10:56.620 --> 10:59.680
Implemented entirely in the keyboard's microcontroller firmware.

10:59.980 --> 11:02.100
And they just renamed it Caps Lock.

11:02.380 --> 11:04.100
It's such a bizarre realization.

11:04.580 --> 11:12.800
Our modern software interfaces are essentially performing a digital pantomime of a mechanical limitation that hasn't existed for a century.

11:12.800 --> 11:15.120
It perfectly illustrates Flyxion's thesis.

11:15.120 --> 11:21.240
The physical constraint vanishes, but the interface it forced into existence hardens into infrastructure.

11:21.580 --> 11:23.040
It just haunts the system forever.

11:23.340 --> 11:24.040
I mean, think about it.

11:24.040 --> 11:27.880
Every single time you accidentally hit Caps Lock and end up yelling in a work email,

11:28.420 --> 11:32.320
you are directly interacting with a 19th century mechanical compromise.

11:32.720 --> 11:33.260
You really are.

11:33.380 --> 11:38.260
You are pushing a button whose sole architectural reason for existing is that 150 years ago,

11:38.580 --> 11:41.560
someone's pinky finger got tired from lifting a chunk of metal.

11:41.740 --> 11:43.600
It's incredible when you frame it like that.

11:43.600 --> 11:48.860
But the essay takes this concept of physical machines shaping our reality even further.

11:49.520 --> 11:53.640
Because it's not just about the buttons we press or the physical fatigue in our hands.

11:53.760 --> 11:56.040
No, it goes much deeper into human cognition.

11:56.260 --> 11:56.560
Right.

11:56.560 --> 12:03.720
It's about how the mechanical rhythms of a machine shape the actual cognitive processes of the person using it.

12:04.040 --> 12:06.900
And this is where we have to talk about the philosopher Friedrich Nietzsche.

12:06.900 --> 12:16.600
The intersection of Nietzsche's philosophy and early typing technology is one of the most profound historical examples of cognitive alignment with a machine.

12:17.160 --> 12:22.520
So to set the stage, in 1882, Friedrich Nietzsche's health is rapidly deteriorating.

12:23.100 --> 12:29.380
Specifically, his eyesight is failing him to the point where writing by hand is causing him agonizing headaches.

12:29.380 --> 12:31.620
Right. He needs a solution to keep writing.

12:31.820 --> 12:37.320
So he actually orders a portable writing ball serial number 125 directly from Malling Hansen.

12:37.420 --> 12:41.240
He was, out of sheer physical necessity, an early adopter of tech.

12:41.700 --> 12:44.000
And initially, the machine served him really well.

12:44.140 --> 12:45.260
He could type without looking.

12:45.560 --> 12:48.400
Yeah, he was able to touch type without straining his eyes.

12:48.480 --> 12:53.780
And he ended up producing roughly 60 manuscripts on that specific writing ball.

12:53.780 --> 12:58.140
But the physical reality of 19th century logistics caught up with it.

12:58.620 --> 13:02.440
The machine was damaged in transit during a move to Genoa, Italy.

13:02.680 --> 13:04.020
Which is tragic in itself.

13:04.260 --> 13:11.140
And then he took it to a local mechanic who supposedly had no idea how to handle a precision Danish instrument.

13:11.600 --> 13:14.100
And the mechanic basically ruined it completely.

13:14.460 --> 13:16.980
A very tragic end for a beautiful piece of engineering.

13:17.620 --> 13:22.980
But just before it was rendered entirely useless, a close friend of Nietzsche's noticed something fascinating.

13:22.980 --> 13:25.300
This was the composer Heinrich Koeslitz, right?

13:25.540 --> 13:26.260
Yes, Koeslitz.

13:26.680 --> 13:30.320
He noticed a shift in the letters and manuscripts Nietzsche had been typing.

13:30.820 --> 13:37.080
Koeslitz wrote to him, suggesting that this new mechanical instrument might actually be altering the shape and cadence of his prose.

13:37.280 --> 13:41.560
He noticed the writing was becoming tighter, more telegraphic, maybe a bit punchier.

13:42.020 --> 13:47.500
And Nietzsche, typing his reply on that very same writing ball, completely agreed with him.

13:47.800 --> 13:49.000
He famously wrote back,

13:49.420 --> 13:51.940
Our writing tools are also working on our thoughts.

13:51.940 --> 13:54.700
Our writing tools are also working on our thoughts.

13:55.320 --> 13:57.960
That is a staggering piece of self-awareness.

13:58.220 --> 13:58.560
It is.

13:59.060 --> 14:05.500
Nietzsche recognized that the interface between himself and the page was not a neutral, invisible conduit.

14:05.980 --> 14:12.140
The constraints, the mechanical resistance, the sheer physical demands of operating the writing ball,

14:12.520 --> 14:14.500
they were all feeding back into his brain.

14:14.600 --> 14:17.520
Altering his cognitive process of composition itself.

14:17.620 --> 14:18.080
Exactly.

14:18.460 --> 14:20.880
He even wrote a poem about his relationship with the machine.

14:20.880 --> 14:22.100
I have it right here.

14:22.440 --> 14:22.800
He wrote,

14:23.240 --> 14:27.140
The writing ball is a thing like me, made of iron, yet easily thrown out of true.

14:27.840 --> 14:32.020
Patience and tact must be had in abundance and fine fingers to make use of us.

14:32.300 --> 14:35.180
It's a very striking, melancholic piece of self-reflection.

14:36.020 --> 14:38.840
The physical alignment to the iron machine, its need for tact,

14:39.260 --> 14:42.900
its susceptibility to being knocked out of true by transit or rough handling,

14:42.900 --> 14:46.680
it perfectly mirrors the fragile cognitive alignment of the writer himself.

14:46.680 --> 14:49.080
The tool dictates the terms of engagement.

14:49.200 --> 14:50.840
But okay, let me push back on this just a bit.

14:50.860 --> 14:51.420
Sure, go ahead.

14:51.660 --> 14:54.940
Because while the poem is highly romantic and sounds very philosophical,

14:55.280 --> 14:57.200
we have to look at the practical reality.

14:58.060 --> 15:02.560
Was Nietzsche really experiencing a profound machine-driven cognitive shift,

15:02.800 --> 15:07.280
or was he just projecting his own miserable mood onto the typewriter?

15:07.760 --> 15:08.600
That's a fair question.

15:08.600 --> 15:13.580
I mean, the guy's going blind, his expensive new gadget got smashed on a train to Genoa,

15:13.820 --> 15:16.440
and a mechanic just destroyed what was left of it.

15:16.840 --> 15:22.160
Doesn't a frustrating broken tool just make you write angrier, choppier prose by default?

15:22.320 --> 15:24.040
Definitely doesn't help his mood, that's for sure.

15:24.120 --> 15:24.340
Right.

15:24.660 --> 15:28.580
Is the machine actually changing the structural grammar of how he thinks,

15:28.660 --> 15:29.840
or is he just highly annoyed?

15:30.300 --> 15:31.860
It's a completely valid skepticism,

15:32.260 --> 15:36.060
and historians and media theorists have debated exactly that for decades.

15:36.060 --> 15:41.700
Was his shift toward an aphoristic, punchy style a result of his philosophical evolution,

15:42.580 --> 15:43.540
his failing health,

15:43.800 --> 15:48.020
or the mechanical limitations of the riding ball forcing him to be brief?

15:48.260 --> 15:49.500
It could be a mix of all three.

15:49.900 --> 15:50.440
Exactly.

15:51.040 --> 15:55.280
But here's why Flyxion includes this historical anecdote in the essay.

15:56.280 --> 15:59.720
The absolute truth of Nietzsche's psychological state

15:59.720 --> 16:04.020
is secondary to the fact that he identified the mechanism of feedback.

16:04.020 --> 16:07.920
Ah, okay, the idea that the feedback loop exists at all.

16:08.120 --> 16:08.320
Yes.

16:09.040 --> 16:11.540
Later media theorists, like Friedrich Kittler,

16:12.000 --> 16:14.740
built entire frameworks on this exact observation,

16:15.140 --> 16:20.060
that the hardware of media fundamentally shapes the discourse it transmits.

16:20.360 --> 16:22.780
And Flyxion is taking that established theory of prose

16:22.780 --> 16:25.160
and applying it strictly to software syntax.

16:25.400 --> 16:25.920
Precisely.

16:26.260 --> 16:29.700
Just as the iron ball left a fingerprint on philosophical prose,

16:29.700 --> 16:35.140
early hardware constraints left a formal structural residue in the grammar of computer code.

16:35.740 --> 16:37.480
The tool always leaves a fingerprint.

16:37.480 --> 16:41.000
Okay, so if a heavy iron ball can leave a fingerprint on human thought,

16:41.480 --> 16:45.580
what happens when you have to translate the actions of that heavy iron mechanism into electricity?

16:45.720 --> 16:47.900
Oh, this is where it gets really technical and fascinating.

16:48.020 --> 16:51.880
Right, because what happens when you have to take the physical movement of a typewriter carriage

16:51.880 --> 16:54.360
and beam it across a telegraph wire?

16:54.360 --> 16:57.600
This brings us to a foundational moment in computing history.

16:58.120 --> 17:02.380
A moment where physical physics hard-coded itself into digital infrastructure.

17:03.120 --> 17:08.480
We have to look at the evolution of that single carriage return button on Molling-Hansen's machine.

17:08.720 --> 17:10.000
The one we talked about earlier.

17:10.200 --> 17:14.420
The one that turned the carriage back to the start and stepped the paper down a line simultaneously.

17:14.420 --> 17:16.380
Yes, that dual action button.

17:16.980 --> 17:21.900
The standard three-row typewriters that dominated the late 19th and early 20th centuries

17:21.900 --> 17:27.460
kept this as one single unified physical action for the human.

17:27.620 --> 17:30.400
Right, it was usually a large metal lever on the side of the carriage

17:30.400 --> 17:33.100
that you'd smack with your hand, just sweeping it all the way to the right.

17:33.220 --> 17:38.700
And for a human typist, merging those two actions into one lever sweep is highly efficient.

17:39.380 --> 17:43.540
But as we moved into the era of the teleprinter, or the teletype,

17:43.540 --> 17:47.980
keeping those actions merged became an insurmountable physical problem.

17:48.660 --> 17:51.040
Because teletypes were electromechanical machines, right?

17:51.380 --> 17:55.460
Designed to send and receive typed messages over telegraph and telephone wires.

17:55.900 --> 17:58.060
But why does a wire make a difference?

17:58.760 --> 18:01.820
If sweeping a lever works for a local typewriter,

18:02.500 --> 18:06.180
why wouldn't one simple electrical signal work for a teletype?

18:06.260 --> 18:08.740
Because of the inescapable physics of inertia.

18:09.000 --> 18:09.720
Inertia.

18:10.000 --> 18:10.380
Yes.

18:10.380 --> 18:14.400
On a receiving teleprinter, you have a heavy physical printhead.

18:15.240 --> 18:18.900
Imagine a solid block of metal containing all the type elements.

18:18.960 --> 18:20.000
Oh, I'm picturing it.

18:20.260 --> 18:24.000
When that printhead reaches the far right edge of a wide roll of paper,

18:24.320 --> 18:26.720
and the sending machine transmits a return signal,

18:27.220 --> 18:33.160
that heavy block of metal has to physically travel all the way back across the rail to the left margin.

18:33.260 --> 18:35.080
Right, it has to slide all the way across.

18:35.280 --> 18:36.580
And that takes time.

18:36.580 --> 18:41.840
Exactly. It takes real physical time. We are talking about hundreds of milliseconds.

18:42.320 --> 18:47.780
Now, to a human, it doesn't sound like much, but in the context of electrical transmission, it is an absolute eternity.

18:47.940 --> 18:53.240
Okay, so if the receiving teletype is processing incoming electrical signals at a high baud rate,

18:53.660 --> 18:58.700
say it's reading the electrical signals way faster than the metal print head can slide across the rail, what happens?

18:58.700 --> 19:03.960
Well, think about the sequence. If the sending machine transmits the return signal,

19:04.700 --> 19:08.120
and then immediately transmits the first letter of the very next sentence.

19:08.240 --> 19:13.320
The receiving machine processes that new letter immediately and attempts to print it.

19:13.400 --> 19:17.920
Right. But because the heavy printhead is still in the middle of this physical journey,

19:18.060 --> 19:22.160
sliding back to the left margin, it fires the hammer in midair.

19:22.360 --> 19:23.160
Oh, no.

19:23.500 --> 19:27.980
Yeah, it prints the first letter of the new sentence smack in the middle of the page while it's still moving.

19:27.980 --> 19:31.820
You get a smeared, diagonally misaligned mess of text.

19:31.980 --> 19:37.760
So the physical reality of momentum and inertia was completely breaking the electrical transmission protocol.

19:37.900 --> 19:38.860
Completely breaking it.

19:39.260 --> 19:43.480
The engineers couldn't change the laws of physics, obviously, so they had to hack the protocol.

19:44.060 --> 19:50.660
They had to figure out how to artificially buy time for the mechanical printhead to finish its physical journey.

19:50.760 --> 19:51.560
And what was their solution?

19:51.560 --> 19:59.220
Their solution was to take that single human action, the carriage return, and split it into two entirely separate electrical signals.

19:59.540 --> 20:00.560
They split the stroke.

20:00.740 --> 20:01.440
Okay, how did that work?

20:01.580 --> 20:03.900
First, they send the carriage return signal.

20:04.360 --> 20:08.420
That explicitly tells the motor to start pulling the heavy print head back to the left.

20:08.460 --> 20:08.920
And then?

20:09.160 --> 20:16.000
Then they send a second distinct signal, line feed, which tells the platen cylinder to rotate and step the paper down one line.

20:16.000 --> 20:18.720
But stepping the paper down doesn't take very long.

20:18.920 --> 20:19.020
Right.

20:19.100 --> 20:23.540
Why does sending the line feed signal second actually solve the inertia problem?

20:23.820 --> 20:37.960
Because the simple act of the sending machine transmitting that second signal and the receiving machine parsing and executing it consumes just enough electrical and mechanical time to allow the heavy carriage to finish sliding all the way to the left.

20:38.060 --> 20:38.920
Ah, I see.

20:38.920 --> 20:41.000
The second signal is essentially a buffer.

20:41.680 --> 20:48.300
It buys the necessary milliseconds for the primary physical action to complete before the first character of the new line arrives.

20:48.440 --> 20:50.020
Here's where it gets really interesting to me.

20:50.540 --> 20:53.700
If I'm understanding this, it's exactly like a track and field event.

20:53.920 --> 20:54.780
Okay, how so?

20:55.020 --> 21:01.560
Imagine a sprinker who has just finished a dash and has to jog all the way back to the starting line for the next heat.

21:02.080 --> 21:05.120
The carriage return signal is the runner heading back to the start.

21:05.120 --> 21:10.580
The line feed signal is the coach intentionally blowing a whistle to stall for a second.

21:10.700 --> 21:11.460
To stall, yes.

21:11.480 --> 21:19.020
Just forcing everyone to wait so the runner actually has time to catch their breath and get their feet set in the blocks before the starting gun fires again.

21:19.100 --> 21:20.780
That is a brilliant analogy.

21:21.240 --> 21:25.040
The line feed was quite literally a mechanical stall whistle.

21:25.040 --> 21:35.660
And this pragmatic physical hack, which, again, was invented just to account for the weight of a middle block, became enshrined in the absolute bedrock of computing.

21:36.000 --> 21:36.700
It got standardized.

21:37.020 --> 21:37.220
Yes.

21:37.540 --> 21:45.140
In 1963, when the American Standard Code for Information Interchange, which we all know is ASCII, was finalized, it didn't just digitize letters and numbers.

21:45.380 --> 21:48.680
It codified this exact physical delay.

21:48.820 --> 21:52.400
It literally encoded the stall whistle into the digital dictionary.

21:52.940 --> 21:53.380
It did.

21:53.380 --> 21:58.560
It assigned carriage return, or CR, to the decimal value 13.

21:59.180 --> 22:04.420
And it assigned line feed, or LF, to the decimal value 10.

22:05.180 --> 22:12.200
Two distinct adjacent control characters inherited directly from the physical necessity of heavy teletype hardware.

22:12.740 --> 22:19.040
And understanding that history explains a massive structural rift in operating systems that we still deal with today, doesn't it?

22:19.140 --> 22:22.120
Oh, it explains so much of modern computing frustration.

22:22.120 --> 22:34.480
Because when the pioneers at Bell Labs, like Ken Thompson and Dennis Ritchie, were developing Unix in the late 1960s and early 70s, they were writing software for computers connected to these exact types of teletype terminals.

22:34.860 --> 22:37.980
But Unix took a very specific philosophical approach.

22:38.200 --> 22:38.400
Right.

22:38.480 --> 22:41.640
The Unix philosophy was all about stripping away unnecessary cruft.

22:41.640 --> 22:47.520
The developers realized that at the software level, a file doesn't need to care about the physical physics of the printer.

22:47.760 --> 22:49.760
The software shouldn't care how heavy the printhead is.

22:49.760 --> 22:56.780
Exactly. So they decided that internally, Unix would only use the line feed character, LF, as its single logical end-of-line marker.

22:57.120 --> 23:00.080
They delegated the messy physical reality to the terminal driver.

23:00.360 --> 23:04.180
The terminal driver being the specific piece of software talking directly to the hardware.

23:04.180 --> 23:14.180
Yes. When the Unix terminal driver saw a line feed in the code, it would dynamically transload it into a carriage return and a line feed for the physical teletype machine.

23:14.440 --> 23:19.180
So Unix cleanly separated the logical text from the physical hardware constraint.

23:19.360 --> 23:20.800
It was very elegant.

23:20.800 --> 23:27.160
But when Microsoft was developing MS-DOS a decade later, they were operating in a completely different market environment.

23:27.640 --> 23:39.000
They were heavily reliant on backward compatibility with older systems like CPM, and they had to interface with a massive, chaotic ecosystem of cheap, third-party serial printers.

23:39.380 --> 23:43.320
And those printers expected explicit, old-school instructions.

23:44.060 --> 23:49.920
Microsoft couldn't rely on sophisticated terminal drivers in the early days of DOS to handle translations on the fly.

23:49.920 --> 23:50.720
So what did they do?

23:50.800 --> 23:55.620
They made the architectural choice to keep both characters explicitly in the actual text files.

23:56.300 --> 24:04.380
MS-DOS, and subsequently Windows, required a carriage return, followed by a line feed CRLF, to signify a new line in a file.

24:04.600 --> 24:07.440
Which means Windows is still blowing the stall whistle today.

24:07.440 --> 24:09.920
Yes. It absolutely is.

24:10.560 --> 24:16.780
Billions of text files sitting on servers and hard drives all over the world right now contain a digital pause.

24:16.780 --> 24:24.220
A character whose original architectural purpose was to give a metal block time to slide across a rail.

24:24.280 --> 24:25.600
That is just mind-blowing.

24:25.600 --> 24:36.960
It codified the very concept of a line in computing, not as a neutral mathematical abstraction, but as a discrete unit of text bounded by a physical hardware limitation.

24:36.960 --> 24:39.940
Which means you are directly affected by this.

24:39.940 --> 24:51.320
Every time you open a text file created on a Mac or a Linux machine, which follow the Unix standard on an older Windows machine, suddenly all the line breaks are completely gone and the text is just one massive, unreadable block of characters.

24:51.600 --> 24:54.940
Or you go the other way and there are weird M characters at the end of every single line.

24:54.940 --> 25:00.960
Yes. And that frustration you feel is a direct disagreement over a 1960s mechanical teletype delay.

25:01.300 --> 25:07.900
Like Flyxion implies, the past is never dead in software. It's not even past. It just sinks to the bottom and becomes infrastructure.

25:08.280 --> 25:17.760
So, once this physical line and its mechanical teletype delays were hard-coded into operating systems, early programmers had to figure out how to actually edit those lines of code.

25:18.040 --> 25:20.700
But they were doing it on those exact same printing terminals.

25:20.760 --> 25:22.620
Right. They didn't have CRT screens yet.

25:22.620 --> 25:30.260
Which forces us to ask an insane question. How do you design a text editor for a human who is effectively blind?

25:30.880 --> 25:33.220
And this is where we dive into the grammar of Ed.

25:33.340 --> 25:33.780
Ed!

25:34.040 --> 25:39.020
Yes, Ed is the original standard line editor for Unix, also written by Ken Thompson.

25:39.600 --> 25:45.620
To really understand its syntax, you have to mentally place yourself in the computing environment of 1971.

25:45.920 --> 25:46.780
Okay, set the scene.

25:46.780 --> 25:52.460
You are sitting at a teletype Model 33. It is a large machine. It is loud.

25:52.620 --> 25:53.540
It vibrates.

25:53.600 --> 25:57.840
And crucially, there is no glowing monitor showing you a nice paragraph of text.

25:58.080 --> 26:07.420
You are typing on a keyboard, and your input, along with the computer's output, is being printed onto a continuous roll of yellow paper by a mechanical print head.

26:07.760 --> 26:13.180
At roughly 10 characters per second, it is an environment of extreme sensory deprivation for the user.

26:13.180 --> 26:18.000
Because you cannot just glance up and see the context of your code. You cannot point a mouse at a typo.

26:18.140 --> 26:19.040
No, absolutely not.

26:19.120 --> 26:19.260
Yeah.

26:19.520 --> 26:28.120
If you want to know what you wrote on line 15, you have to type a specific command to ask the computer to physically print line 15 onto the paper in front of you.

26:28.260 --> 26:29.900
You are literally editing in the dark.

26:29.900 --> 26:31.260
Yes, editing in the dark.

26:31.320 --> 26:38.500
And because of that brutal physical constraint, Ed had to develop a very specific, unforgiving grammatical rule.

26:39.480 --> 26:42.340
Flyxion highlights this as the core thesis of the essay.

26:42.860 --> 26:46.500
The one massive rule of Ed is address before command.

26:46.840 --> 26:49.500
Address before command. What does that mean in practice?

26:49.500 --> 26:59.000
In practice, this means that before you tell the editor what you want to do, the verb or the command, you must first mathematically declare where you want to do it.

26:59.440 --> 27:00.120
The address.

27:00.340 --> 27:05.580
So if I realize I made a mistake and I want to delete lines 3 through 5, I can't just type delete 3 to 5.

27:05.640 --> 27:08.620
No, the machine won't understand that. You have to type 3000 5P.

27:08.880 --> 27:12.040
The 3000 is the address. The D is the command for delete.

27:12.160 --> 27:13.420
Address first, verb second.

27:13.860 --> 27:17.760
Profacely. If you want to insert text before line 1, you type 1i.

27:17.760 --> 27:20.180
1 being the line, i being insert.

27:20.460 --> 27:29.260
Yes. If you want to substitute a word on the current line, you provide the address of the line and then the substitute command, formatted like sold word, new word.

27:29.720 --> 27:33.360
The syntax demands the location first and the verb second, without exception.

27:33.960 --> 27:40.440
To survive in this dark environment, early developers built an incredibly robust vocabulary just for addressing things, didn't they?

27:40.520 --> 27:42.280
They had to. It was their only way to navigate.

27:42.600 --> 27:45.780
So a bare number jumped you to a specific line.

27:45.780 --> 27:51.380
A dollar sign meant the last line of the document, so you didn't have to know how long the file actually was.

27:51.480 --> 27:53.360
A period meant the current line you were on.

27:53.560 --> 27:59.120
Right. And you could do relative math, like plus 5 or n of 3, to jump forward or backward from your current position.

27:59.480 --> 28:07.440
And you could use slashes to search forward using regular expressions like apple or questionmarks.apple to search backward.

28:07.620 --> 28:10.060
It's like having a whole math language just to find your text.

28:10.060 --> 28:14.500
And ed also enforced a strict modality, which is critical to understand.

28:14.840 --> 28:26.080
It split the user's interaction into a command mode, where you are entering these addresses and verbs, and an input mode, where you are actually typing the raw text you want to insert into the file.

28:26.080 --> 28:35.660
And the only way to escape input mode and get back to commanding the editor was to type a single lone period on a completely empty line and hit return.

28:36.160 --> 28:42.900
It's a brilliant workaround for the time, but you really have to imagine the intense cognitive load that placed on the programmer.

28:43.260 --> 28:44.580
You weren't just writing code.

28:44.980 --> 28:50.640
You were playing a relentless game of mental chess against your own fragile working memory.

28:50.820 --> 28:52.240
I have to play devil's advocate here.

28:52.300 --> 28:52.440
Sure.

28:52.440 --> 28:55.360
This sounds like an incredibly clunky way to work.

28:55.500 --> 28:57.460
Why didn't they just design it the other way around?

28:57.620 --> 28:59.160
Why not command before address?

28:59.580 --> 29:02.020
Why not let the user type D345?

29:02.880 --> 29:07.420
Was it a technical limitation of the PDP-11 computer?

29:07.680 --> 29:09.480
Like the computer's parser couldn't handle it?

29:09.500 --> 29:10.180
Yeah, exactly.

29:10.460 --> 29:15.140
Did the computer need to load the memory address into RAM before it could process the delete instruction?

29:15.260 --> 29:16.780
That is the instinctual assumption, right?

29:16.820 --> 29:16.940
Yeah.

29:17.160 --> 29:19.220
But Flyxion's research makes it totally clear.

29:19.220 --> 29:22.580
No, it was not a technical limitation of the computer architecture.

29:23.180 --> 29:27.120
The parser inside Ed could easily have been written to accept D335.

29:27.620 --> 29:30.700
Yeah, the program maintained an internal pointer to the current line.

29:31.100 --> 29:34.220
It could execute the logic regardless of the syntax order.

29:34.360 --> 29:34.540
Yeah.

29:34.640 --> 29:36.120
The computer absolutely did not care.

29:36.240 --> 29:36.680
Okay, wait.

29:37.040 --> 29:40.860
If the computer's memory didn't care, why force the human to do it backward?

29:41.520 --> 29:43.100
Because human memory did care.

29:43.100 --> 29:56.240
If we look at cognitive load theory, specifically George Miller's famous 1956 paper on the limits of human working memory, where he found we can only hold about seven items in our short-term memory.

29:56.700 --> 29:57.940
The magical number seven rule.

29:58.060 --> 29:58.560
Exactly.

29:58.960 --> 30:02.880
When you look at that, the syntax of Ed makes perfect psychological sense.

30:03.440 --> 30:10.920
When you are editing blind without a screen, orientation and targeting become the exact same cognitive process.

30:10.920 --> 30:14.880
Right, because if I can't see the screen, I don't just need to figure out what word I want to delete.

30:15.140 --> 30:20.220
I have to mentally calculate the mathematical coordinates of where it is before I can even touch it.

30:20.420 --> 30:20.820
Exactly.

30:21.400 --> 30:26.120
Let's say you were typing away and you realize you need to delete a flawed function you wrote 10 minutes ago.

30:26.660 --> 30:31.000
Your brain has to stop and reconstruct a mental model of the unseen document.

30:31.140 --> 30:32.260
Which takes a lot of effort.

30:32.480 --> 30:33.900
It takes immense mental effort.

30:34.400 --> 30:36.640
You think, okay, what lines was that function on?

30:36.640 --> 30:39.380
I think it started around line 40 and ended around line 45.

30:39.940 --> 30:47.240
That piece of calculated information, the address 40,045, is highly volatile in your working memory.

30:47.600 --> 30:48.820
It is so easy to forget.

30:49.060 --> 30:50.240
Ah, I get it now.

30:50.720 --> 30:56.800
The address is expensive to compute in your head, while the command like delete is incredibly cheap and obvious.

30:56.940 --> 30:58.620
You already know you want to delete something.

30:59.020 --> 30:59.660
Spot on.

30:59.660 --> 31:08.540
So, the grammar requires you to put the address first, because it is the piece of information most likely to slip away if you get distracted by figuring out the command syntax.

31:08.780 --> 31:15.080
You write down where you are before you declare what you want to do, because the machine offers zero visual help to remind you.

31:15.240 --> 31:15.700
Exactly.

31:16.420 --> 31:24.320
The syntax is designed to offload the cognitive burden from the human's fragile short-term memory directly onto the command line as quickly as possible.

31:24.700 --> 31:26.820
You lock in the coordinates before they evaporate.

31:26.820 --> 31:30.920
It is defensive design against human forgetfulness in a dark environment.

31:31.520 --> 31:37.900
The epistemology of the user, what the user is capable of knowing at any given moment, dictates the grammar of the tool.

31:38.240 --> 31:41.500
It's a perfect synthesis of human limitation and interface design.

31:41.760 --> 31:46.640
But what happens to this rigid, survival-based grammar when the lights finally turn on?

31:46.860 --> 31:50.720
What happens when the glass teletype arrives and you can actually see your text?

31:51.000 --> 31:55.560
This is where the narrative shifts from historical constraint to interface evolution.

31:55.560 --> 32:01.120
As we moved into the late 1970s and 80s, CRT monitors became standard.

32:01.700 --> 32:03.700
You had a persistent, glowing screen.

32:04.000 --> 32:04.840
No more rolling paper.

32:04.980 --> 32:05.500
No more paper.

32:05.980 --> 32:13.160
This led to the development of full-screen editors, primarily VI, written by Bill Joy at Berkeley and its modern descendant, VIM.

32:13.480 --> 32:18.240
And with VIM, you have a persistent, two-dimensional display mapped directly to a memory buffer.

32:18.400 --> 32:18.560
Right.

32:18.660 --> 32:22.020
The computer is constantly, dynamically showing you the context of your code.

32:22.600 --> 32:25.120
The user always knows exactly where they are for free.

32:25.120 --> 32:31.900
And because the screen provides that spatial orientation for free, the brutal cognitive constraint that's shaped dead completely vanishes.

32:32.600 --> 32:37.100
The working memory is no longer burdened with holding those voluble numerical coordinates.

32:37.460 --> 32:43.220
And VIM responds to this new environmental reality by doing something structurally profound.

32:43.220 --> 32:49.840
In its primary mode of interaction, called normal mode, it completely inverts the grammar.

32:50.060 --> 32:53.100
It switches from address before command to command before address.

32:53.240 --> 32:54.240
It flips the script.

32:54.380 --> 32:55.840
It completely flips the script.

32:55.960 --> 32:59.320
In VIM's normal mode, the rule is generally operator motion.

33:00.060 --> 33:03.280
The operator is the verb and the motion is the address.

33:03.280 --> 33:06.620
So if you want to delete to the end of a paragraph, you type D.

33:06.820 --> 33:07.100
Right.

33:07.440 --> 33:12.100
The D is the command for delete, and the address is the address for end of paragraph.

33:12.600 --> 33:15.220
If you want to delete a specific word, you type DW.

33:16.060 --> 33:19.620
If you want to change the text inside a pair of quotes, you type C.

33:20.460 --> 33:21.660
Notice the grammatical shift.

33:21.940 --> 33:23.000
The voob comes first.

33:23.340 --> 33:24.540
The addressa comes second.

33:24.540 --> 33:31.020
From a computer science perspective, under the hood, it's about how the parser builds its abstract syntax tree.

33:31.660 --> 33:35.760
When you type DGOM, the editor enters an operator-pending state.

33:36.140 --> 33:39.320
It knows you want to delete something, but it halts and waits for the object.

33:39.740 --> 33:42.380
It's a highly object-oriented approach to text editing.

33:42.580 --> 33:43.980
The verb is waiting for a known.

33:44.360 --> 33:47.500
But why is this inversion so important?

33:47.500 --> 33:54.460
I mean, if the address first way was so deeply ingrained in Unix culture, why change it just because you have a monitor?

33:54.920 --> 34:01.600
Because, as Flyxion argues, when the screen is providing constant visual feedback, your location is never lost.

34:01.760 --> 34:01.940
Yeah.

34:02.080 --> 34:07.420
You don't have to spend your first burst of mental effort desperately clinging to numerical coordinates.

34:07.660 --> 34:09.320
You can just look at the blinking cursor.

34:09.520 --> 34:11.680
So your cognitive load is totally freed up.

34:11.880 --> 34:12.340
Exactly.

34:12.680 --> 34:19.140
Therefore, your first keystroke can be spent declaring your intent rather than purchasing your orientation.

34:19.480 --> 34:20.960
Intent before orientation.

34:21.140 --> 34:21.860
I want to delete.

34:22.060 --> 34:22.340
Yes.

34:22.700 --> 34:23.580
You announce your intention.

34:23.740 --> 34:24.040
Delete.

34:24.540 --> 34:25.180
The editor waits.

34:25.780 --> 34:28.660
Then you provide the address, the motion to confirm the scope.

34:29.060 --> 34:29.620
This word.

34:30.720 --> 34:33.840
The address is no longer a desperate tool for finding yourself in the dark.

34:34.060 --> 34:36.820
It becomes a simple, localized parameter for the action.

34:37.440 --> 34:38.500
So what does this all mean?

34:38.560 --> 34:40.420
It's like sitting at a table in a restaurant, right?

34:40.680 --> 34:41.600
I love this analogy.

34:41.720 --> 34:41.980
Go ahead.

34:42.260 --> 34:44.460
Normal mode is when the food is right in front of you.

34:44.540 --> 34:46.800
You don't need to give the waiter GPS coordinates.

34:47.020 --> 34:48.360
You just declare your intent.

34:48.500 --> 34:49.200
I'm going to eat.

34:49.420 --> 34:50.160
That's the command.

34:50.160 --> 34:53.040
And you point your fork at the burger on your plate.

34:53.480 --> 34:54.360
That's the address.

34:54.680 --> 34:59.060
Command before address because the object is visible and immediately accessible.

34:59.060 --> 35:00.400
A perfect metaphor.

35:00.400 --> 35:00.860
Yeah.

35:00.860 --> 35:06.380
And this persistent visual display allowed for something that was conceptually impossible in Ed.

35:06.960 --> 35:07.780
Visual mode.

35:07.780 --> 35:12.840
Visual mode is the ability to physically highlight text on the screen before you act on it.

35:12.920 --> 35:16.460
You cannot highlight text on a piece of paper rolling out of a teletype machine.

35:16.460 --> 35:21.360
Highlighting requires a memory duffer that is constantly redrawing a screen state.

35:21.640 --> 35:22.080
Precisely.

35:22.660 --> 35:26.760
Visual mode is the ultimate expression of the screen taking over the work of addressing.

35:27.500 --> 35:34.460
You visually select the area using arrow keys or motions, defining the address entirely through visual interface feedback,

35:34.920 --> 35:38.160
and then you just hit a single command key like D to delete it.

35:38.720 --> 35:40.680
The visual selection becomes the address.

35:40.680 --> 35:47.280
But, and this is where Vim reveals its true architectural brilliance, it didn't entirely abandon the old ways, did it?

35:47.360 --> 35:48.300
No, it didn't.

35:48.400 --> 35:51.960
Vim still contains a massive, fully functional fossil.

35:52.180 --> 35:53.080
It's called X-Mode.

35:53.200 --> 35:53.460
Yes.

35:53.980 --> 35:56.160
X-Mode, which you access by typing a colon.

35:56.620 --> 36:01.300
If you type .305d in Vim, it deletes lines 3 through 5.

36:01.720 --> 36:06.040
If you type .sol.noog, it substitutes text globally across a line.

36:06.180 --> 36:08.020
Wait, that is exactly Ed's grammar.

36:08.400 --> 36:09.820
Address first, then command.

36:09.820 --> 36:12.860
It kept the old syntax completely verbatim.

36:13.120 --> 36:15.780
The exact same letters, the exact same structure.

36:15.920 --> 36:16.260
It did.

36:16.660 --> 36:21.240
And the critical analytical question, fluctuated dosage, is why keep both?

36:21.440 --> 36:21.660
Yeah.

36:21.780 --> 36:26.180
Why have two entirely different grammatical structures operating inside the exact same program?

36:26.620 --> 36:29.680
Is it just stubborn historical baggage from older programmers?

36:30.140 --> 36:32.080
The essay argues it is not baggage at all.

36:32.160 --> 36:33.200
It is fiercely logical.

36:33.380 --> 36:36.980
Because what happens when you are editing a file with 10,000 lines of code?

36:37.120 --> 36:37.480
Exactly.

36:37.480 --> 36:41.580
If you are looking at line 10 on your screen, but you realize you need to delete lines 800

36:41.580 --> 36:44.420
through 850, those lines are not on your monitor.

36:44.620 --> 36:45.240
You can't see them.

36:45.320 --> 36:46.640
You can't point your cursor at them.

36:46.700 --> 36:48.080
You can't highlight them in visual mode.

36:48.440 --> 36:51.100
Relative to that specific action, you are blind again.

36:51.480 --> 36:55.480
The screen is no longer providing you with spatial orientation for those specific lines.

36:55.480 --> 37:02.180
So, when you are editing off-screen, your epistemological state reverts perfectly back to the constraints of ed.

37:02.620 --> 37:03.600
You are back in the dark.

37:04.040 --> 37:04.320
Yes.

37:04.960 --> 37:08.800
When the visual orientation is gone, the cognitive burden returns.

37:09.340 --> 37:11.860
You have to mentally calculate where those lines are.

37:12.220 --> 37:17.020
And because that calculation is volatile in your working memory, the old rule instantly applies.

37:17.020 --> 37:20.440
The command must supply the location first.

37:20.620 --> 37:25.920
You have to specify the address, .800,850- before you specify the command.

37:25.920 --> 37:33.360
To go back to your restaurant analogy, if normal mode is eating the burger on the plate in front of you, X mode is ordering delivery.

37:33.560 --> 37:33.960
Oh, wow.

37:34.140 --> 37:34.220
Yes.

37:34.220 --> 37:35.080
You can't see the food.

37:35.200 --> 37:35.700
It's off-screen.

37:36.080 --> 37:37.700
So, you have to provide the address first.

37:37.800 --> 37:38.980
Go to 123 Main Street.

37:39.200 --> 37:41.120
And then you give the command, drop off the burger.

37:41.560 --> 37:47.800
You can't say drop off the burger at 123 Main Street in the same instinctual, immediate way you'd point to a plate.

37:47.800 --> 37:50.280
But you have to establish the remote reality first.

37:50.740 --> 37:54.560
It is a profound observation about the deep structure of interface design.

37:55.460 --> 37:58.640
Vim didn't just arbitrarily pick a dual syntax to annoy people.

37:59.000 --> 38:02.920
It sorted its grammatical systems strictly based on one variable.

38:03.560 --> 38:08.820
Whether the screen is doing the work of locating the target or if the human brain has to do it.

38:09.300 --> 38:13.060
The grammar adapts perfectly to the epistemological state of the user.

38:13.380 --> 38:13.860
Can you see it?

38:14.700 --> 38:15.480
Operator first.

38:15.640 --> 38:16.240
Are you blind?

38:16.500 --> 38:17.380
Address first.

38:17.800 --> 38:21.960
It is an incredibly elegant piece of logic hiding in plain sight.

38:22.420 --> 38:29.200
It really makes you realize that the software tools we use every day are constantly having these silent conversations with our cognitive women.

38:29.260 --> 38:30.020
We absolutely are.

38:30.020 --> 38:39.200
I mean, think about how the menus in your word processor or your video editor change or how the keyboard shortcuts shift depending on whether an item is currently selected on your screen or not.

38:39.380 --> 38:45.020
The visibility of the digital object fundamentally dictates the grammar of how you are allowed to manipulate it.

38:45.020 --> 38:50.300
We are all speaking this language of visual constraint without even realizing we are bilingual.

38:50.300 --> 38:57.300
However, Flyxion is careful to note in the final sections of the essay that we shouldn't become too deterministic in our thinking.

38:58.120 --> 39:05.240
Not everything in modern software is merely a rearranged fossil of old mechanical or cognitive constraints.

39:05.240 --> 39:11.160
Right, because if we stop here, it's easy to assume that all software is just a museum of old typewriters and blind terminals.

39:11.700 --> 39:16.760
But the essay highlights a specific feature that represents a clean break from the past, doesn't it?

39:16.760 --> 39:17.400
It does.

39:17.400 --> 39:24.620
As compute power and memory capacity exploded exponentially, software architectures begin to break the lineage.

39:25.040 --> 39:29.920
And the evolution of the undo command is the perfect counterexample to formal residue.

39:30.320 --> 39:31.120
Let's talk about undo.

39:31.360 --> 39:40.340
Because in ED, operating on a machine with mere kilobytes of RAM, the undo feature, which was just triggered by the letter U, is incredibly primitive.

39:40.340 --> 39:47.180
It is effectively a single toggle. It reverses the last command you executed by simply swapping pointers in a temporary buffer.

39:47.380 --> 39:53.080
The essay calls it an involution over one remembered state. It's basically a Boolean flip, a light switch.

39:53.520 --> 39:58.400
You are in state A, you make a mistake and enter state B. You press U, you go back to state A.

39:58.680 --> 40:06.920
But if you press U again, it doesn't take you back to a previous state C. It just undoes the undo, dropping you right back into state B.

40:06.920 --> 40:12.500
It's a severely bounded mechanism, dictated entirely by the hardware limitations of early computers.

40:13.160 --> 40:16.440
They simply didn't have the memory to store a deep history of file states.

40:16.940 --> 40:22.360
But Vim's undo mechanism, developed years later as RAM became plentiful, is entirely different.

40:22.640 --> 40:27.880
It doesn't just rearrange the old grammar, it introduces a conceptually unbounded capability.

40:28.280 --> 40:33.140
Vim's undo isn't just a stack of actions where you hit C to go back, back, back.

40:33.140 --> 40:40.280
It is a persistent branching tree. It is a directed authentic graph. Every single change you make is a node.

40:40.660 --> 40:45.420
Right. And if you undo five steps backward and then type something new, you don't overwrite the old future.

40:45.640 --> 40:51.660
You create a brand new branch in time. You have multiple navigable futures and pasts stored in the computer's memory.

40:51.660 --> 40:55.160
It is essentially localized time travel with multiverse branching.

40:55.560 --> 41:03.160
It is an infinitely more complex, mathematically abstract mechanism that the developers of Ed could never have dreamed of supporting on a PDP-11.

41:03.580 --> 41:05.900
Flyxion uses this to provide essential nuance.

41:06.500 --> 41:13.040
When hardware finally allowed it, when we transcended the physical constraints of memory and processing power software developers,

41:13.600 --> 41:19.160
introduced entirely novel, unbounded mechanisms that broke free from the mechanical past.

41:19.160 --> 41:23.180
So software evolution is basically this constant schizophrenic tug of war.

41:24.160 --> 41:29.420
Is it safe to say then that half of the code base is dragging around the heavy iron ghosts of 1870,

41:29.820 --> 41:35.040
ensuring the mechanical teletype delays are strictly respected so old text files don't break,

41:35.200 --> 41:40.480
and the other half of the code base is casually inventing branching, multidimensional time travel?

41:40.660 --> 41:42.380
That is exactly the dynamic.

41:42.860 --> 41:47.020
We are building hyper-futuristic capabilities on top of 19th century bedrock.

41:47.020 --> 41:50.100
And the architectural joints where those two paradigms meet,

41:50.460 --> 41:56.640
where the multiverse time machine has to eventually hit a standard Ackie carriage return to display text on a screen,

41:57.280 --> 42:00.180
that is where interface design becomes a profound study of human history.

42:00.400 --> 42:03.140
Let's pull back and look at the sheer scope of this journey.

42:03.580 --> 42:06.520
We started over 150 years ago in a workshop in Denmark,

42:07.020 --> 42:10.500
looking at a brass writing ball built into a wooden desk with a foot pedal.

42:10.760 --> 42:12.400
Yep, the mechanical multi-tool.

42:12.400 --> 42:19.000
We saw how the heavy physical effort of shifting a typewriter carriage forced the invention of a physical latch,

42:19.160 --> 42:23.600
which still haunts our modern keyboards today as the digital caps lock key.

42:24.160 --> 42:29.840
We watched that single physical carriage return action get split into two separate electrical signals,

42:30.360 --> 42:35.120
carriage return and line feed, just to stall a teletype machine's heavy metal print head.

42:35.240 --> 42:35.880
The stall whistle.

42:35.880 --> 42:43.460
We saw how ASCII codified that physical delay, creating a digital riff that still breaks text files between Mac and Windows today.

42:43.940 --> 42:48.640
We saw how that physical line dictated how blind text editors like Ed had to work,

42:48.900 --> 42:54.920
forcing humans to type their address first to offload the immense cognitive burden of navigating in the dark.

42:55.040 --> 43:01.520
And finally, we saw how a program like Vim flipped that entire grammar on its head the moment computer monitors turned on.

43:01.520 --> 43:09.240
Allowing us to declare intent before orientation while still keeping the old address first ways in X mode for whenever we have to edit off screen.

43:09.240 --> 43:10.840
It's a breathtaking lineage.

43:11.420 --> 43:15.280
And this raises an important question, or rather the most important takeaway for anyone listening.

43:15.980 --> 43:23.680
The line of text you type on today, the syntax of the terminal commands you use, the layout of the keyboard under your fingers,

43:23.940 --> 43:27.760
none of it is a neutral, mathematically perfect abstraction.

43:28.080 --> 43:28.680
No, it's not.

43:28.680 --> 43:31.460
It is the encoded residue of physical friction.

43:32.120 --> 43:37.220
It is the memory of heavy iron, of teletype motors, and of human cognitive limitations.

43:37.800 --> 43:42.820
You are operating a living museum of past constraints every single time you open a laptop.

43:43.160 --> 43:45.360
The physical world never really left our software.

43:45.680 --> 43:46.540
It just went undercover.

43:46.720 --> 43:47.460
It went undercover.

43:47.740 --> 43:53.620
And that leaves me with one final provocative thought for you, the listener, to mull over as you go about your day.

43:53.620 --> 44:02.720
If the heavy iron typewriter, the tired pinky finger, and the blind teletype terminal secretly wrote the digital grammar of the 20th and 21st centuries,

44:03.140 --> 44:07.580
what physical constraints are writing the rules for the 22nd century right now?

44:07.680 --> 44:08.940
That's the million-dollar question.

44:08.940 --> 44:20.160
When we use a flat glass touchscreen with zero tactile feedback, or when we rely on swipe keyboards that track the continuous messy drag of a thumb across glass,

44:20.320 --> 44:25.640
or when we increasingly dictate out loud into tiny ambient voice assistant microphones,

44:26.440 --> 44:29.880
what invisible grammar are we locking into place for the future?

44:30.180 --> 44:31.540
It's hard to even imagine.

44:31.540 --> 44:37.200
Long after the smartphone is sitting behind glass in a museum right next to Molly Hansen's writing ball,

44:37.620 --> 44:42.700
what digital ghost of a thumb swipe, or what artificial pause born from a voice command latency,

44:42.920 --> 44:48.660
will our great-grandchildren still be stuck using, wondering why their holographic spatial interfaces act so strangely?

44:48.940 --> 44:50.580
We are laying down the bedrock right now.

44:50.680 --> 44:52.860
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.

44:53.220 --> 44:56.960
Keep questioning the invisible rules of the tools you use every day, and we'll see you next time.

